1 NOTE
2 CONT Pg 12 of Gerhard Naeseth and Blaine Hedberg's Emigration to United States 1849.  Thor Faa sailed on the Ebenezer from Stavanger, Rogaland about April 26 or 28, 1849 and arrived in New York on June 13, 15, or 18th, 1849.   H. C. Cl
2 CONC ausen, Captain.  About two thirds of the way to America, the Ebenezer encountered a hurricane.
2 CONT
2 CONT #153 - Thore Olsen Faa (Thore Olsen, 21, m), son of Ole Sørensen Få and Kari Thorsdatter born in Finnøy, Rogaland, December 30, 1827. Emigrated from Finnøy, Rogaland to LaSalle County, Illinois; 1853 to Preble Township, Fillmore C
2 CONC ounty, MN.  Married ca. 1850, Elisabeth Christiansdatter Spande (1849:164).  Children include:  Ole, born ca. 1850, died by 1863; Anna Karina, born May 12, 1852, died Feb 6, 1909; Christian, born Oct 22, 1854, died March 27, 1933;
2 CONC Siri, born September 22, 1856, died August 30, 1930; Asseline, born October 2 or 20 1858; Gustava, born November 22, 1860; Ole, born May 21, 1863, died May 24, 1942; Theodor, born May 29, 1865, died July 18, 1957; Elias, born Augu
2 CONC st 8, 1867, died April 4, 1950; Josef, born March 27, 1870, died March 20, 1963.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thore traveled to Finnøy in 1893 and purchased a part of the Få farm. He returned to Finnøy in 1896 and was married there on April 29, 1896 to Anna Kristine Torgersdatter Hesby, born 1849.  Thore died in Norway on September 2, 190
2 CONC 2 and is buried in the Hesby churchyard.  His second wife, Anna Kristine Torgersdatter died in 1928 -- Finnøy Gard og ætt, Volume II, 378-379; Neswburg (Scheie) Lutheran Church Records; Dennis Spande.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg 309 - Fillmore County History - 1982 - Thor Olson Faa, father of Elias Faa, was an immigrant from Stavanger, Norway.  He first settled in Illinois, but decided to move westward with the hope of getting a claim and building a ho
2 CONC me.  He came to Preble Section 33 and settled; conditions seemed favorable to him as there was water, wood and hay ground.  Their possessions were three yoke oxen, a few cattle and two rickety lumber wagons and a few household goo
2 CONC ds.  A burning rag in a pan of grease gave them their first light.  Later they made tallow candles. The cloth for the clothing was home spun by the women.  In recording their claim, three items were necessary; give proper location
2 CONC , be 21 yeas of age and build a house.  A law office was organized in Brownsville, MN where he went to have his claim recorded.  He paid only $1.25 an acre. Christian Faa, son of Thor, was the first white child born in Preble Town
2 CONC ship.  Thor had a family of ten children.  Elias Faa, who was the son of Thor, followed in his father's footsteps.  He married Margaret Holland and they were blessed with six children.  Thomas Faa married Julia Erickson and they h
2 CONC ad two children.  Julius Faa married Minnie Veum and they had eight children, Lennie, Leslie, Marian, Leland, Laverne, Walter, Violet, Gloria, Elmer Faa who married Geva Iverson, had two children, Norman and Carla. Emma Faa marrie
2 CONC d Loren and they had seven children, Arden, Norris, Mervin, Lyle, Phyllis, Alene and Janice.  Grace Faa married Henry Eddy and had three children, Robert, Margie and Joan. Luella Faa died at an early age.  Elias was a progressive 
2 CONC farmer and was a promoter of church activities.  He built many new buildings on the farm, especially the large, spacious house surrounded with beautiful trees and a well kept yard.  They lived their whole life there and area burie
2 CONC d in the Scheie Cemetery north of Mabel, Minnesota.  Elias died in 1950 and his wife, Margaret died in 1960.  The farm is located half a mile west of the Scheie church.  Elmer Faa and son Norman and his family were the last to liv
2 CONC e on the homestead which they left in 1963.
